(Quarterly Research Brief: Retrieval-Augmented Generation in Practice) Tj T*
T*
(1. Background) Tj T*
(Retrieval-augmented generation \(RAG\) pairs a language model with an external) Tj T*
(knowledge base. The model retrieves relevant passages at query time instead of) Tj T*
(relying only on parameters learned during training.) Tj T*
T*
(2. Findings) Tj T*
(Teams that adopted RAG reported a 38 percent drop in unsupported answers.) Tj T*
(The largest gains came from documents that change frequently, such as policies) Tj T*
(and product manuals. Freshness mattered more than model size.) Tj T*
T*
(3. Recommendations) Tj T*
(Start with a narrow corpus, measure answer groundedness, and add citations.) Tj T*
(Review chunking strategy quarterly. Scanned documents still need OCR before) Tj T*
(they can be indexed reliably.) Tj T*
T*
(4. Conclusion) Tj T*
(RAG is not a replacement for evaluation. It is a way to keep answers tied to) Tj T*
(sources that a reviewer can inspect.) Tj T*
ET
